Part 4 - Preparing the Data

Before using the dataset for Logistic Regression, I would prepare the data so that the model can properly understand and use it.

Missing Values:
I checked the dataset for missing values and found that there were no missing values. Because of this, I did not need to remove any rows or
fill in any missing information.

Categorical Variables:
Some columns contain categories instead of numbers. These include education, self_employed, and loan_status. Since Logistic Regression works
with numerical data, I would convert these categories into numbers. For example, self_employed could be changed to 1 for Yes and 0 for No. 
The target variable loan_status could also be changed to 1 for Approved and 0 for Rejected.

Non-Numeric Information:
The main non-numeric information comes from the categorical columns mentioned above. I would change these values into numbers so they can be
used by the model. I would make sure the original meaning of each category stays the same after converting it.

Duplicate Records:
I would check the dataset for duplicate rows before training the model. If I found any exact duplicates, I would remove them because having
the same row more than once could affect the model and give certain observations more importance than they should have.

Unnecessary Columns:
I would remove the loan_id column before training the model. This column is only used to identify each loan application and does not provide
useful financial information about the applicant. Including it could add unnecessary information to the model.

Other Data-Quality Issues:
I would also check the numerical columns for unusual or incorrect values. For example, I would look for negative values where they would not
make sense, such as a negative income or loan amount. I would also make sure the data types are correct and that categorical values are
written consistently.
